      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the binomial distribution formula, which is given by:\n\nP(x) = C(n, x) * p^x * (1-p)^(N-x)\n\nwhere:\nx - the number of successes (in this case, rolling an even number on a die)\nn - the number of trials (in this case, rolling 5 dice)\np - the probability of success (in this case, the chance of rolling an even number on a single die)\nN - the total number of trials (in this case, the total dice rolled)\n\nThe even numbers on a 12-sided die are 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12. So, the probability of rolling an even number on a single die is p.\n\nWe are asked to find the probability that exactly two of the five dice show an even number. So, the value of x is 2.\n\nSince we are rolling the dice 5 times, the total number of trials is N = 5.\n\nPutting it all together, we get:\n\nP(2) = C(5, 2) * (1/6)^2 * (5/6)^3\n\nLet's calculate this step by step:\n\nC(5, 2) = \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = 10\n\n(1/6)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{36}\n\n(5/6)^3 = \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^3 = \\frac{125}{216}\n\nNow, we multiply these values together to find the probability:\n\nP(2) = 10 * \\frac{1}{36} * \\frac{125}{216} = \\frac{1250}{7776} \\approx 0.1607\n\nSo, the probability that exactly two of the dice show an even number is \\boxed{\\frac{1250}{7776}}.",
